# The daily O2PDPSuite release, built for slc10. Jenkins cuts the tag and builds
# it for slc9; this builds the same tag for slc10.
#
# One regex: it selects the tag AND names its parts. %(date) is today, so only
# the release for the current day is ever offered.
#
# Only the _TEST cuts to begin with -- the dailies come in two forms,
# O2PDPSuite-daily-20260825-0000 and O2PDPSuite-daily-20260825-1100_TEST, and
# until slc10 releases are trusted we build the latter. Make the group optional
# ((?P<variant>_TEST)?) to take every cut.
#
# Quoted because the value contains parentheses -- unquoted it is a bash syntax
# error, and since these files are sourced the check would simply never appear.
CI_NAME=build_O2PDPSuite_daily_slc10
PACKAGE=O2PDPSuite
PR_REPO=alisw/alidist
PR_BRANCH=master
ALIBUILD_DEFAULTS=o2
CHECK_NAME=release/O2PDPSuite/daily-slc10-x86

# What the tag name means. Named groups become TAG_<NAME> in the build
# environment (TAG_SUITE, TAG_CHANNEL, TAG_DATE, TAG_TIME, TAG_VARIANT), so the
# build can name what it publishes after the release rather than after the day
# it happened to run. A tag that does not match is a hard failure -- see
# build-one.sh for why.
# Anchored with a LITERAL up to and including the date. list-release-tags asks
# GitHub for the leading literal run of this regex, so starting it with a
# capture group -- (?P<suite>...) -- leaves nothing to ask for and pulls all
# 10514 alidist refs, ~5 MB, every round. The suite and channel are constants
# here anyway; only the cut time and the variant vary.
TAG_REGEX='^O2PDPSuite-daily-%(date)-(?P<time>[0-9]{4})(?P<variant>_TEST)?$'
DONT_USE_COMMENTS=1

# REBUILD_TAGS deliberately NOT set. It was O2PDPSuite-daily-* during the
# bring-up, which forces the tag for that day to be offered even when it already
# carries a status -- including the *pending* one an aborted build leaves
# behind, which nothing else ever clears. Leaving it set means every worker
# session rebuilds the release for that day.
#
# Set it again, temporarily, to re-run a tag: a rebuild is cheap, since
# everything it needs comes back out of the CAS.
